</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Common Commercial Claim Issues</h2>
<h3>Business income and business interruption</h3>
<p>Business income coverage is designed to replace lost net income and continuing expenses while a covered loss is repaired. Disputes often center on the length of the period of restoration, the waiting period, and how projected sales are calculated. Clean financial records make this part of the claim far easier to support.</p>
<h3>Coinsurance</h3>
<p>If the building is underinsured relative to the coinsurance percentage on the declarations, the payment may be reduced. Some policies use an agreed value endorsement that suspends coinsurance. Checking the insurer's valuation of the building is an important step.</p>
<h3>Scheduled vs. blanket limits</h3>
<p>A scheduled policy assigns a separate limit to each building or location. A blanket policy shares one limit across several locations. On a large storm that hits multiple buildings, this difference can affect how much is available for any one structure.</p>
<h3>Tenant vs. owner responsibilities</h3>
<p>Leases often split responsibility: the owner insures the building, and the tenant insures its improvements and betterments, inventory, and equipment. When both file after the same event, the claims should be coordinated so items are not omitted or claimed twice.</p>
<h3>Code upgrades on older buildings</h3>
<p>The City of McAllen adopted the 2024 ICC codes effective January 1, 2026. Repairs may trigger code requirements, but payment for them depends on ordinance or law coverage. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes</a> page.</p>

<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<p>Commercial settlements are often paid in stages. Many replacement-cost policies pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are complete, often within a deadline. Business income may be paid in partial advances as records are reviewed. Large or complex scopes sometimes need a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> when hidden damage appears during repairs. If the dispute is only about the amount of loss, the policy may allow <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a>. You can test rough numbers with our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> before you talk to the carrier.</p>

