<h2>How a Commercial Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>Joseph Dittman, a licensed Texas public adjuster, and our public adjusters represent the policyholder only. On commercial claims, that work may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reviewing the policy, endorsements, and statement of values before the inspection</li>
<li>Inspecting the building and preparing a line-item estimate in Xactimate</li>
<li>Organizing business income and extra expense documentation with your accountant</li>
<li>Coordinating with tenants, property managers, and HOA boards</li>
<li>Meeting the insurer's adjusters and engineers and responding to information requests</li>
<li>Reviewing the carrier's estimate; see our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> service</li>
</ul>
<p>A public adjuster cannot guarantee a result, and coverage always depends on the policy and the facts. Public adjusters are not attorneys; if a claim moves toward litigation, consult a Texas attorney. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Report the loss promptly and in writing.</li>
<li>Take reasonable steps to prevent further damage, such as tarping, water extraction, and securing openings, and keep the receipts.</li>
<li>Keep damaged materials or photograph them before disposal.</li>
<li>Provide requested records, including financials for business income, within a reasonable time.</li>
<li>Submit a sworn proof of loss if the policy or insurer requires it.</li>
<li>Cooperate with inspections and examinations allowed by the policy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations</h2>
<p>Commercial forms vary widely, and endorsements can change the base form. Common limitations include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flood exclusion</strong> on most standard commercial property forms.</li>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductibles</strong> stated as a percentage of the building value, sometimes applied per building.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusions</strong> for metal roofing and siding on some policies.</li>
<li><strong>Wear and tear, deterioration, and maintenance</strong> exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Vacancy provisions</strong> that can limit coverage when a building has been vacant for a stated period.</li>
<li><strong>Sublimits</strong> for items such as signs, outdoor property, debris removal, and ordinance or law.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hidalgo County is not in the TWIA catastrophe area, so McAllen businesses typically carry wind and hail coverage through their regular commercial insurer. If you are unsure what your policy includes, a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> is a good starting point. For a broader look at storm documentation, read <a href="/document-commercial-property-damage-after-storm/">how to document commercial property damage after a storm</a>.</p>
